window.IG_COURSE=[{"week":1,"title":"Replicator Equations and Scalar Dynamics","subtitle":"Competition, proportions, and equilibria","summary":"Connect population proportions, phase lines, vector-field graphs, and time-dependent solutions in a visual study of competition.","reading":"readings/week01.html","demo":"demos/week01.html","lab":["A mixed population begins with 48 individuals of type A and 72 of type B. Later it contains 90 of type A and 180 of type B. Compute the total size and the proportion of A at each time. Did the number of A individuals increase? Did A's proportion increase? Explain why those conclusions differ.","Suppose \\(N_A'=f_A N_A\\) and \\(N_B'=f_B N_B\\). Starting with \\(x=N_A/(N_A+N_B)\\), use the quotient rule to derive \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\). Then suppose the fitness advantage is the constant \\(f_A-f_B=1.5\\). Identify the equilibria and sketch a phase line on \\([0,1]\\).","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\): (a) find all equilibria in \\([0,1]\\); (b) calculate the signs of \\(x'\\) at \\(x=0.25,\\;0.60,\\;0.90\\); (c) draw a phase line with arrows and classify each equilibrium; (d) explain the population meaning of the stable equilibrium.","Open the synchronized demo. Select \"Stable coexistence,\" set \\(x(0)=0.20\\), and advance to \\(t=4\\). Record \\(x(t)\\) and \\(x'(t)\\), then explain how these values appear in the phase line, vector-field graph, and solution graph. Repeat with \\(x(0)=0.90\\). Use the three pictures to explain why both solutions approach the same equilibrium.","Select \"Bistability (threshold)\" in the demo. Run from \\(x(0)=0.30\\) and \\(x(0)=0.70\\). Record their long-term trends. Then examine \\(x'=x(1-x)(2-5x)\\): find the interior equilibrium, determine stability using a sign chart, and calculate \\(F'(x^*)\\). Explain how the local derivative relates to what you see in the solution curve."],"quiz":[["A population contains 20 A individuals and 30 B individuals. What is the proportion \\(x\\) of type A?",["\\(\\frac25\\)","\\(\\frac23\\)","\\(\\frac35\\)","\\(\\frac12\\)"],0,"Divide the count of A by the total: 20/(20+30)=2/5."],["For \\(0<x<1\\), if \\(f_A>f_B\\), what happens to the proportion of A?",["It decreases.","It increases.","It stays constant.","It becomes larger than 1."],1,"Inside the interval x(1−x)>0, so the sign of x′ is the sign of the fitness difference."],["For \\(x'=2x(1-x)\\), which equilibrium attracts all interior initial conditions?",["x=0","x=1","x=1/2","There is no equilibrium."],1,"The derivative is positive throughout the interior, so trajectories move toward 1."],["What are the equilibria of \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\)?",["\\(0,\\;\\frac12,\\;1\\)","\\(0,\\;\\frac34,\\;1\\)","\\(\\frac14,\\;\\frac34\\)","\\(0,\\;1\\) only"],1,"Set each of the three factors equal to zero."],["At \\(x=0.90\\) for \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), what is the sign of \\(x'\\)?",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Cannot be determined"],2,"The factors x and 1−x are positive, while 3−4(0.90) is negative."],["Which graph has time on its horizontal axis?",["The graph of \\(F(x)=x'\\) against x","The solution graph \\(x(t)\\)","The phase line","The graph of the two fitness functions versus x"],1,"A solution graph follows one initial condition through time. The vector-field graph uses state x on the horizontal axis."],["For \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), what is \\(F'(3/4)\\)?",["\\(-\\frac34\\)","\\(\\frac34\\)","0","3"],0,"At the interior root, F′=x(1−x)(−4)=−(3/4)(1/4)4=−3/4."]],"sections":[["From populations to proportions","Let \\(N_A(t)\\) and \\(N_B(t)\\) denote two populations, growing according to \\(N_A'=f_A N_A\\) and \\(N_B'=f_B N_B\\). Their growth rates can depend on population composition. Define the fraction \\(x=N_A/(N_A+N_B)\\). By the quotient rule,\n\\[\nx'=\\frac{N_A'N_B-N_AN_B'}{(N_A+N_B)^2}\n=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B).\n\\]\nThe absolute population size disappears: this equation describes relative abundance. It is called the two-strategy replicator equation."],["A familiar special case","If the fitness difference is a positive constant \\(k\\), then \\(x'=kx(1-x)\\). Its equilibria are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and every solution initially between them increases toward \\(1\\). Separation of variables gives\n\\[\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{1+\\bigl((1-x_0)/x_0\\bigr)e^{-kt}}.\n\\]\nIt has the logistic form, but here \\(1\\) means complete dominance by type A, not a carrying capacity."],["Frequency-dependent fitness","Suppose \\(f_A=3-2x\\) and \\(f_B=2x\\). Then \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\). The zeros are \\(0,\\;3/4,\\;1\\). For \\(0<x<3/4\\), the derivative is positive; for \\(3/4<x<1\\), it is negative. Hence the interior equilibrium \\(x^*=3/4\\) attracts interior trajectories. With \\(F(x)=3x-7x^2+4x^3\\), we obtain \\(F'(3/4)=-3/4\\), so nearby perturbations satisfy approximately \\(u'=-3u/4\\)."]],"key":"x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(2-5x)\\), the equilibria are \\(0,2/5,1\\). The sign of \\(x'\\) is positive below \\(2/5\\) and negative above it, so \\(2/5\\) is asymptotically stable for interior initial conditions.","readingPath":"readings/week01.html","demoPath":"demos/week01.html","labPath":"labs/week01.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week01.html"},{"week":2,"title":"Payoff Matrices and Stability","subtitle":"From interactions to the entire phase portrait","summary":"Use a 2×2 payoff matrix to derive expected fitness, predict invasion and stability, and investigate bifurcations.","reading":"readings/week02.html","demo":"demos/week02.html","lab":["A population uses strategies A and B with payoff matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Write \\(f_A(x)\\) and \\(f_B(x)\\) as weighted averages and simplify. (b) Evaluate both at \\(x=1/4\\) and \\(x=9/10\\). (c) From these two states, which direction does A’s proportion move? (d) Explain how the fitness-line intersection predicts an equilibrium.","Derive the equation \\(x'=x(1-x)\\left[(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\right]\\). For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&3\\\\2&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), compute the fitness difference, draw a phase line, and describe the long-term population composition from any interior initial state. Confirm using the demo’s A-dominance preset.","Consider the coordination matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Find the interior equilibrium and classify all equilibria on \\([0,1]\\). (b) Use the demo with initial proportions \\(x(0)=0.35\\) and \\(x(0)=0.65\\). (c) Explain what separates their long-term outcomes. (d) Sketch the two solution curves on the same axes.","Fix \\(b=4,\\;c=3,\\;d=1\\) while changing \\(a\\) from 2 to 4. Use the demo’s \\(a\\)-slider. At \\(a=2,\\;2.8,\\;3,\\;3.5\\), record the equilibrium locations and their stability. Determine algebraically the parameter at which stable coexistence disappears into the boundary. Explain what the phase line looks like at the critical parameter.","Begin with \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). Use the demo to compare the original model with (i) the matrix formed by adding 1 to every payoff and (ii) the matrix formed by multiplying every payoff by 2. Show algebraically which transformation leaves the differential equation unchanged. For the other transformation, identify what changes and what remains the same about the trajectories."],"quiz":[["For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the expected fitness of strategy A is",["\\(ax+b(1-x)\\)","\\(cx+d(1-x)\\)","\\(a+b\\)","\\(ax+cx\\)"],0,"Weight A's payoffs against A and B by their frequencies x and 1−x."],["For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the difference \\(f_A-f_B\\) equals",["\\(1-2x\\)","\\(3-4x\\)","\\(4x-3\\)","\\(2x+1\\)"],1,"Here f_A=4−2x and f_B=1+2x; subtract."],["When A is very rare, what determines whether its proportion initially increases?",["\\(a>c\\)","\\(a>b\\)","\\(b>d\\)","\\(c>d\\)"],2,"At x=0 the fitness difference is b−d."],["Which endpoint signs indicate stable interior coexistence?",["\\(\\Delta(0)>0,\\;\\Delta(1)<0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)<0,\\;\\Delta(1)>0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)>0,\\;\\Delta(1)>0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)<0,\\;\\Delta(1)<0\\)"],0,"The advantage must change from positive on the left to negative on the right."],["For the coordination matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the interior equilibrium is",["\\(x^*=3/4\\), stable","\\(x^*=1/2\\), stable","\\(x^*=1/2\\), unstable","\\(x^*=1/4\\), unstable"],2,"The fitness difference is −2+4x. Its sign changes from negative to positive at 1/2."],["For an interior equilibrium \\(p\\) of \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(\\alpha+\\beta x)\\), which expression equals \\(F'(p)\\)?",["\\(p(1-p)\\beta\\)","\\(\\alpha+\\beta\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\alpha\\)","\\(0\\) always"],0,"Differentiate the product. At p, the factor α+βp is zero."],["What happens if the same constant is added to every entry of the payoff matrix?",["Both strategies immediately disappear.","The interior equilibrium always moves to 1/2.","The entire replicator equation stays unchanged.","The direction of every trajectory reverses."],2,"Each expected fitness increases by the same amount, so their difference is unchanged."]],"sections":[["Interactions become expected fitness","With proportion \\(x\\) of strategy A and payoff matrix \\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}\\), random encounters give expected fitness \\(f_A(x)=ax+b(1-x)\\) and \\(f_B(x)=cx+d(1-x)\\). Row entries are the payoffs received by the corresponding strategy, while columns identify opponents. The replicator equation remains \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\)."],["The difference is a line","Subtracting gives \\(\\Delta(x)=f_A-f_B=(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\), so \\(x'=x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\). The endpoint values are \\(\\Delta(0)=b-d\\) and \\(\\Delta(1)=a-c\\). A rare type A grows in frequency when \\(b>d\\); a rare type B grows in frequency when \\(c>a\\). These signs determine the arrows on the phase line."],["Four qualitative cases","If \\(\\Delta\\) is positive throughout \\((0,1)\\), A dominates; if negative, B dominates. If it changes from positive to negative, an interior equilibrium attracts, giving stable coexistence. If it changes from negative to positive, an unstable interior equilibrium separates two basins of attraction, called bistability or coordination. If \\(\\Delta\\) vanishes at an endpoint, use a sign chart; if it vanishes everywhere, every state is stationary."],["Linearization and parameters","Write \\(\\alpha=b-d\\), \\(\\beta=a-b-c+d\\), so \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(\\alpha+\\beta x)\\). At an interior equilibrium \\(p=-\\alpha/\\beta\\), \\(F'(p)=p(1-p)\\beta\\). Also \\(F'(0)=b-d\\) and \\(F'(1)=c-a\\). For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), \\(\\Delta(x)=3+(a-6)x\\), and the interior attracting state reaches \\(x=1\\) when \\(a=3\\). This is a bifurcation."]],"key":"x'=x(1-x)\\left[(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\right]","example":"For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the expected fitnesses are \\(f_A=4-2x\\) and \\(f_B=1+2x\\). Hence \\(\\Delta(x)=3-4x\\), with stable coexistence at \\(x^*=3/4\\). By contrast, \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\) gives \\(\\Delta=-2+4x\\) and an unstable threshold \\(x^*=1/2\\).","readingPath":"readings/week02.html","demoPath":"demos/week02.html","labPath":"labs/week02.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week02.html"},{"week":3,"title":"Three Strategies and the Probability Simplex","subtitle":"From the interval to a triangle","summary":"Derive a three-strategy replicator system and explore invariant faces, cyclic competition, and planar stability.","reading":"readings/week03.html","demo":"demos/week03.html","lab":["A population has proportions \\(\\mathbf p=(0.50,0.30,0.20)\\). (a) Explain why it is a point in the triangle \\(\\Delta^2\\). (b) Identify the missing strategy along each of the three edges. (c) Give an example of a tangent velocity vector whose coordinates sum to zero, and describe which strategies gain and lose share. (d) In the demo, drag the initial point to a new interior location and estimate its three proportions.","Derive the three-strategy replicator equation. Begin with \\(N_i'=f_iN_i\\), set \\(p_i=N_i/N\\), and calculate \\(N'/N\\). Then, for \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), compute \\(\\overline f\\) and \\(\\mathbf p'\\). Verify that the velocity is tangent to the simplex.","Consider the cyclic payoff model with \\(s=1\\). Start with \\(\\mathbf p(0)=(0.4,0.6,0)\\), exactly on the AB edge. (a) Write the fitnesses on that edge. (b) Reduce the system to an equation for \\(p_A\\) and predict which pure state attracts the edge trajectory. (c) Observe this in the simplex demo. (d) Repeat with \\(\\mathbf p(0)=(0.4,0.59,0.01)\\). Why can the second trajectory explore the interior while the first cannot?","Set the cyclic loss magnitude to \\(s=0.7\\), then \\(s=1\\), then \\(s=1.3\\), using the same initial point \\((0.55,0.25,0.20)\\). For each model, sketch the trajectory on the triangle, describe the time series, and record how \\(P=p_Ap_Bp_C\\) changes. Derive \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\ln P=(1-s)(1-3Q)\\), where \\(Q=p_Ap_B+p_Bp_C+p_Cp_A\\), and use it to explain all three observations.","Use \\(x=p_A\\) and \\(y=p_B\\), so \\(p_C=1-x-y\\). The reading gives the Jacobian at the central equilibrium for the cyclic model: \\(J_s=\\frac13\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}s&s+1\\\\-(s+1)&-1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Compute its trace and determinant. (b) Classify the equilibrium for \\(s=0.7,\\;1,\\;1.3\\) using the eigenvalues. (c) Compare the Cartesian phase-plane view to the barycentric triangle view in the demo. Explain why they describe the same planar system."],"quiz":[["Which point lies in the standard three-strategy probability simplex?",["\\((0.2,0.3,0.5)\\)","\\((0.4,0.4,0.4)\\)","\\((-0.1,0.5,0.6)\\)","\\((1,1,0)\\)"],0,"All components must be nonnegative and their sum must equal 1."],["How many independent coordinates are needed to describe a three-strategy probability vector?",["One","Two","Three","Four"],1,"Normalization imposes one independent linear constraint on three coordinates."],["If \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), what is the mean fitness?",["1.0","1.5","1.9","2.5"],2,"Compute the weighted average: 0.2·3+0.3·1+0.5·2=1.9."],["Under replicator dynamics, the sum \\(p_A'+p_B'+p_C'\\) is",["Equal to 1","Equal to mean fitness","Equal to 0","Always negative"],2,"The derivative of p_A+p_B+p_C=1 is 0, and the replicator terms cancel."],["If \\(p_C(0)=0\\), what happens to C under the smooth replicator equation?",["Its proportion becomes positive immediately","Its proportion stays zero","The state leaves the simplex","Both remaining proportions become zero"],1,"The right-hand side of C's equation has a factor p_C, so the edge is invariant."],["For \\(M_s=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&-s\\\\-s&0&1\\\\1&-s&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), which state is the interior equilibrium?",["\\((1,0,0)\\)","\\((1/2,1/2,0)\\)","\\((1/3,1/3,1/3)\\)","\\((0,0,0)\\)"],2,"At the uniform composition, all three strategies have equal fitness."],["For the balanced cyclic model \\(s=1\\), the product \\(p_Ap_Bp_C\\) along a nonstationary interior trajectory is",["Strictly increasing","Strictly decreasing","Constant","Always zero"],2,"The logarithmic derivative contains the factor 1−s and therefore vanishes when s=1."],["For \\(s=0.7\\), trajectories near the central equilibrium generally",["Spiral outward","Spiral inward","Remain stationary at every point","Leave the triangle in finite time"],1,"The real part of the central Jacobian eigenvalues is (s−1)/6<0, and the product of proportions increases."]],"sections":[["The simplex as state space","A probability state is \\(\\mathbf p=(p_A,p_B,p_C)\\) with nonnegative coordinates summing to 1. Its space is a triangle, \\(\\Delta^2\\). The triangle has two independent coordinates because \\(p_C=1-p_A-p_B\\); its vertices are pure states and its edges are two-strategy mixtures."],["The three-strategy system","If \\(N_i'=f_iN_i\\), then differentiating \\(p_i=N_i/\\sum_jN_j\\) gives \\(p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\overline f)\\), where \\(\\overline f=\\sum_jp_jf_j\\). On the simplex, \\(\\sum_i p_i'=0\\), so velocities are tangent to the plane of normalized states. If \\(p_k=0\\), then \\(p_k'=0\\); each edge is invariant under smooth replicator dynamics."],["Cyclic competition and circulation","For \\(M_s=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&-s\\\\-s&0&1\\\\1&-s&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the fitness vector is \\(M_s\\mathbf p\\), and \\(\\mathbf p^*=(1/3,1/3,1/3)\\) is an equilibrium. When \\(s=1\\), interior trajectories are closed orbits around that center. If \\(s<1\\), the center attracts; if \\(s>1\\), it repels and trajectories wind outward toward the boundary."],["A quantity that explains stability","For positive proportions, let \\(P=p_Ap_Bp_C\\) and \\(Q=p_Ap_B+p_Bp_C+p_Cp_A\\). The cyclic model obeys \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\ln P=(1-s)(1-3Q)\\), and \\(Q\\leq1/3\\). Thus \\(P\\) increases for \\(s<1\\), is constant for \\(s=1\\), and decreases for \\(s>1\\) away from the central equilibrium. In the coordinates \\(x=p_A,y=p_B\\), the central Jacobian has eigenvalues \\(\\lambda_\\pm=(s-1)/6\\pm i\\sqrt3(s+1)/6\\)."]],"key":"p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\overline f),\\qquad \\overline f=\\sum_jp_jf_j","example":"For \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), mean fitness is \\(1.9\\). Hence \\(\\mathbf p'=(0.22,-0.27,0.05)\\), whose coordinates sum to zero. The state remains on the normalized probability plane.","readingPath":"readings/week03.html","demoPath":"demos/week03.html","labPath":"labs/week03.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week03.html"},{"week":4,"title":"Probability Distributions as States","subtitle":"Random variables, expectation, variance, and curves of distributions","summary":"Interpret simplex points as probability laws, explore Bernoulli and categorical families, and compare exact probabilities with samples.","reading":"readings/week04.html","demo":"demos/week04.html","lab":["A measuring device reports outcomes A, B, or C with probabilities \\((1/2,1/3,1/6)\\). The corresponding readings are \\(0,1,3\\). (a) Verify that the probabilities define a distribution. (b) Draw a probability bar graph with properly labeled numerical outcomes. (c) Calculate the mean and variance using the definitions, and interpret why the mean need not be a possible reading.","Let \\(X\\) have the Bernoulli distribution \\(P(X=1)=p\\). (a) Starting from \\(E[X]\\) and \\(E[X^2]\\), derive \\(E[X]=p\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)\\). (b) Find the maximum variance using calculus. (c) In the demo, compare \\(p=0.1,0.5,0.9\\), recording the two probabilities, mean, and variance for each.","Fix numerical outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), and use categorical states \\(P=(0.6,0.3,0.1)\\) and \\(Q=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\). (a) Calculate the expectation and variance for P and Q. (b) Define \\(P(t)=(1-t)P+tQ\\) and find its velocity. (c) Use the demo's interpolation mode, observe the trajectory on the triangle, and derive \\(dE_{P(t)}[Z]/dt\\). (d) Does the variance change linearly in t? Verify algebraically.","Select the categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p=(0.5,1/3,1/6)\\) (or enter decimal approximations). Draw 10 samples with the demo, then 100 more, and then 1000 more without changing the distribution. Record the empirical frequencies after each stage. Explain why the results change between runs, what the empirical frequencies estimate, and why we should not expect exact agreement even after 1000 observations.","Using the categorical model with outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), set \\(p_A=0.35\\), \\(p_B=0.4\\), and \\(p_C=0.25\\). (a) Write its expectation as \\(\\mu(x,y)=3-3x-2y\\) in coordinates \\(x=p_A,\\ y=p_B\\). (b) Find \\(\\partial\\mu/\\partial x\\) and \\(\\partial\\mu/\\partial y\\). (c) Consider the tangent vector \\(v=(-0.1,0.05,0.05)\\). Show that its coordinates sum to zero, calculate the directional derivative of the expectation, and describe the change in the probability bars."],"quiz":[["A categorical probability distribution must have",["nonnegative probabilities summing to 1","numerical outcomes summing to 1","equal probability for every outcome","exactly two outcomes"],0,"Probabilities are nonnegative and normalized; outcome values may be arbitrary."],["A random observation from a three-category distribution is",["a whole probability vector","one realized outcome, such as B","always the most likely category","the expectation of the distribution"],1,"A model assigns chances; sampling produces one category."],["For \\(z=(0,1,3)\\) and \\(p=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\), the expectation equals",["\\(\\frac56\\)","\\(\\frac43\\)","\\(\\frac12\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{6}\\)"],0,"Compute 0·1/2 + 1·1/3 + 3·1/6 = 5/6."],["For the same distribution, the variance equals",["\\(\\frac{25}{36}\\)","\\(\\frac{41}{36}\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{6}\\)","\\(\\frac56\\)"],1,"The second moment is 11/6; subtract the square of the mean, 25/36."],["For a Bernoulli random variable with \\(P(X=1)=p\\), its variance is",["\\(p\\)","\\(1-p\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\)","\\(p^2\\)"],2,"Because X²=X on outcomes {0,1}, variance is E[X]−E[X]²."],["At which Bernoulli parameter does variance reach its maximum?",["\\(p=0\\)","\\(p=\\frac14\\)","\\(p=\\frac12\\)","\\(p=1\\)"],2,"Differentiate p(1−p) or complete the square."],["A differentiable categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p(t)\\) obeys which identity?",["\\(\\sum_i p_i'=1\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_i'=0\\)","\\(p_i'=p_i\\) always","\\(\\sum_i p_i^2=1\\)"],1,"Differentiate the normalization condition ∑ p_i(t)=1."],["When repeatedly sampling independently from a fixed probability distribution, the empirical proportions",["must equal the exact probabilities after 100 samples","usually differ from the probabilities but tend toward them with large sample size","form a vector whose entries sum to 2","are always constant across repeated experiments"],1,"Sample frequencies are random; the law of large numbers describes their convergence as sample size grows."]],"sections":[["A distribution describes probabilities","A categorical distribution is a vector \\(\\mathbf p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)\\), where the components are nonnegative and sum to one. It describes the probabilities of possible observations, rather than a particular observed outcome. For three categories, the space of distributions is the simplex \\(\\Delta^2\\)."],["Numerical random variables and moments","If the categories have numerical values \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_n\\), expectation is \\(E_{\\mathbf p}[Z]=\\sum_i z_i p_i\\). Writing \\(\\mu=E[Z]\\), the variance is \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=\\sum_i p_i(z_i-\\mu)^2=\\sum_i z_i^2 p_i-\\mu^2\\). For \\(z=(0,1,3)\\) and \\(\\mathbf p=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\), we get \\(E[Z]=5/6\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=41/36\\)."],["Bernoulli distribution family","The Bernoulli family has possible outcomes 0 and 1 with probabilities \\(1-p,p\\). Thus \\(E[X]=p\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)\\), maximized at \\(p=1/2\\). As the parameter changes, the distribution moves along a one-dimensional interval of statistical states."],["Curves and samples in the simplex","A differentiable curve of distributions \\(\\mathbf p(t)\\) satisfies \\(\\sum_i p_i'(t)=0\\). For fixed numerical outcomes, \\(\\frac{d}{dt}E[Z]=\\sum_i z_i p_i'(t)\\). Linear interpolation \\(P(t)=(1-t)P_0+tP_1\\) remains normalized and has constant velocity \\(P_1-P_0\\), but need not solve the replicator equation. For \\(n\\) independent samples, the empirical distribution is \\(\\widehat p_i=n_i/n\\), and typically differs from the underlying \\(\\mathbf p\\)."]],"key":"E_{\\mathbf p}[Z]=\\sum_i p_i z_i,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(Z)=\\sum_i p_i z_i^2-\\left(\\sum_i p_i z_i\\right)^2","example":"For \\(P=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\) and outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), the mean is \\(0(1/2)+1(1/3)+3(1/6)=5/6\\). The second moment is \\(1/3+9/6=11/6\\). Thus the variance is \\(11/6-25/36=41/36\\).","readingPath":"readings/week04.html","demoPath":"demos/week04.html","labPath":"labs/week04.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week04.html"},{"week":5,"title":"Entropy, Log-Odds, and Exponential Updating","subtitle":"The bridge from replicator dynamics to information theory","summary":"Measure surprisal and Shannon entropy, transform replicator equations with log-odds, and solve constant-fitness dynamics by exponential normalization.","reading":"readings/week05.html","demo":"demos/week05.html","lab":["For events of probability \\(1/2\\), \\(1/4\\), and \\(1/10\\), compute surprisal in both nats and bits. Draw or describe how \\(I(p)=-\\ln p\\) changes as \\(p\\) moves toward 0 and 1. Then suppose A and B are independent events with probabilities \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/5\\): compute the surprisal of their joint occurrence and verify that it equals the sum of the individual surprisals.","For the Bernoulli family \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), write Shannon entropy \\(H(p)\\), calculate \\(H'(p)\\) and \\(H''(p)\\), and determine the location and value of its maximum. Use the demo to compare \\(p=0.1,\\;0.5,\\;0.9\\). Why is uncertainty symmetric between p and 1−p? Convert the maximum from nats to bits.","For \\(x'=kx(1-x)\\) with \\(k=1.2\\) and \\(x(0)=1/4\\), find the log-odds \\(L(0)\\), derive \\(L(t)=L(0)+kt\\), and solve for \\(x(t)\\). Use the binary-dynamics demo to verify that log-odds form a straight line in time even though \\(x(t)\\) is curved. What is the time at which the population first reaches \\(x=1/2\\)?","Investigate \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) starting at \\(x(0)=0.1\\). Use the demo to locate where Shannon entropy increases, reaches a maximum, and decreases; sketch both \\(x(t)\\) and \\(H(x(t))\\). Derive \\(\\dot H=\\ln((1-x)/x)x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) and use signs to explain the changing behavior. Compare the stable equilibrium \\(x^*=3/4\\) with the entropy maximum at \\(x=1/2\\).","For three strategies with constant fitnesses \\(f=(1,0,-1)\\) and initial distribution \\(p(0)=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\), derive the exponential normalization formula and compute the normalized weights at \\(t=1\\). Verify that the components sum to one. Use the demo's three-strategy mode to compare \\(p(t)\\) and entropy at \\(t=0,1,4\\). Prove that adding the same constant to all three fitnesses does not change the normalized distribution, and explain why the model is different from the frequency-dependent cyclic system of Week 3."],"quiz":[["For an event with probability \\(p\\), surprisal in nats is",["\\(-\\ln p\\)","\\(\\ln p\\)","\\(p\\ln p\\)","\\(1/p\\)"],0,"Surprisal is −ln p, positive for probabilities strictly between zero and one."],["For two independent events, the surprisal of their joint occurrence is",["the product of their surprisals","the sum of their surprisals","always zero","the larger surprisal only"],1,"Independence multiplies probabilities, and the logarithm turns a product into a sum."],["Shannon entropy of \\(P=(1/2,1/2)\\) in bits is",["0 bits","1 bit","2 bits","\\(\\frac12\\) bit"],1,"Each event has surprisal one bit, so the average is one bit."],["Bernoulli Shannon entropy \\(H(p)\\) is maximized at",["\\(p=0\\)","\\(p=1/4\\)","\\(p=1/2\\)","\\(p=1\\)"],2,"H′=ln((1−p)/p) vanishes at p=1/2 and H″ is negative."],["For \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\), the derivative of \\(L=\\ln[x/(1-x)]\\) is",["\\(x(1-x)\\)","\\(f_A-f_B\\)","\\(\\ln(x)\\)","\\(H(x)\\)"],1,"Differentiate the log-odds and cancel the factor x(1−x)."],["With a constant fitness difference, what happens to log-odds as time passes?",["They follow a sinusoid","They are constant for any nonzero fitness difference","They change linearly with time","They must be bounded between 0 and 1"],2,"The log-odds derivative is the constant fitness difference."],["For constant fitnesses \\(f_i\\), the replicator solution is",["\\(p_i(t)=p_i(0)+f_it\\)","\\(p_i(t)=\\frac{p_i(0)e^{f_it}}{\\sum_jp_j(0)e^{f_jt}}\\)","\\(p_i(t)=e^{f_it}\\)","\\(p_i(t)=f_i/\\sum_jf_j\\)"],1,"Exponentially grow each initial weight, then normalize the weights to sum to one."],["Along a stable replicator trajectory, Shannon entropy",["always increases","always decreases","can increase and later decrease","must remain equal to its maximum"],2,"Entropy measures uncertainty, while asymptotic stability concerns motion toward an equilibrium."]],"sections":[["Surprisal and Shannon entropy","The surprisal of an outcome with probability \\(p>0\\) is \\(I=-\\ln p\\) nats. For independent events, surprisals add because probabilities multiply. Shannon entropy is the expected surprisal \\(H(\\mathbf p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i\\), with \\(0\\ln0=0\\). Entropy in bits uses \\(\\log_2\\) instead of the natural logarithm."],["Binary entropy and calculus","For a Bernoulli distribution, \\(H(x)=-x\\ln x-(1-x)\\ln(1-x)\\), with \\(H'(x)=\\ln((1-x)/x)\\) and \\(H''(x)=-1/[x(1-x)]\\). Its maximum is \\(\\ln2\\) nats at \\(x=1/2\\). For n categories, the uniform distribution maximizes entropy at \\(\\ln n\\) nats."],["Log-odds and the replicator equation","For \\(0<x<1\\), define \\(L=\\ln(x/(1-x))\\). If \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\), then \\(L'=x'/[x(1-x)]=f_A-f_B\\). For a constant fitness difference \\(k\\), \\(L(t)=L(0)+kt\\), and \\(x(t)=1/[1+((1-x_0)/x_0)e^{-kt}]\\). Thus a logistic trajectory becomes a straight line in log-odds coordinates."],["Exponential normalization and entropy change","For constant strategy fitnesses \\(f_i\\), \\(p_i(t)=p_i(0)e^{f_it}/\\sum_jp_j(0)e^{f_jt}\\). This is exponential weighting followed by softmax-like normalization. But Shannon entropy need not grow: along \\(x'=x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\), \\(\\dot H=\\ln((1-x)/x)\\,x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\), which may change sign during the trajectory."]],"key":"H(\\mathbf p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i,\\qquad \\frac{d}{dt}\\ln\\frac{x}{1-x}=f_A-f_B","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) with \\(x(0)=0.1\\), the state moves toward \\(3/4\\). Its Shannon entropy first increases as it passes toward \\(1/2\\), reaches its maximum \\(\\ln2\\) there, then decreases on the way to the stable equilibrium. Stability does not require Shannon entropy to increase.","readingPath":"readings/week05.html","demoPath":"demos/week05.html","labPath":"labs/week05.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week05.html"},{"week":6,"title":"Relative Entropy, Stability, and the Geometry Ahead","subtitle":"KL divergence as an equilibrium-adapted Lyapunov function","summary":"Understand KL as excess log loss, derive its monotone decay along stable replicator flows, and identify the quadratic form that leads to Fisher geometry.","reading":"readings/week06.html","demo":"demos/week06.html","lab":["Start with two Bernoulli distributions \\(Q=(3/4,1/4)\\) and \\(P=(1/2,1/2)\\). (a) Calculate \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) and \\(D(P\\|Q)\\) in nats. (b) Explain why they differ even though they compare the same two points. (c) Compute \\(H(Q)\\) and the cross-entropy \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)\\), then verify \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)-H(Q)=D(Q\\|P)\\). (d) In the binary demo, compare the two divergences for \\(q=0.75,\\;x=0.5\\).","Prove Gibbs' inequality for strictly positive finite distributions. Begin with \\(\\ln u\\leq u-1\\) and substitute \\(u=p_i/q_i\\). Multiply by \\(q_i\\), sum, and use normalization. State precisely when equality holds. Then describe what happens to \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) when Q assigns positive probability to an outcome that P declares impossible.","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), let \\(q=3/4\\) and \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)\\). (a) Differentiate V with respect to x and simplify to \\((x-q)/[x(1-x)]\\). (b) Apply the chain rule to prove \\(\\dot V=-4(x-q)^2\\). (c) In the binary explorer, start at \\(x(0)=0.1\\). Explain why Shannon entropy first increases then decreases while V always decreases. (d) Relate the derivative calculation to asymptotic stability.","In the categorical explorer, set the reference \\(Q=(0.5,0.3,0.2)\\) and the proposed model \\(P=(0.15,0.20,0.65)\\). (a) Compute \\(D(Q\\|P)\\), \\(D(P\\|Q)\\), and the expected log loss of using P when Q generates observations. (b) Define \\(P(t)=(1-t)P+tQ\\) and evaluate the forward KL divergence at \\(t=0,\\;0.5,\\;1\\). (c) Use the simplex heatmap to interpret the minimum. (d) Explain why this interpolation does not need to solve the replicator equation.","Consider a reference Bernoulli probability q and a small displacement h. (a) Write \\(D(q\\|q+h)\\) and show that its first derivative in h vanishes at 0. (b) Calculate the second derivative at 0 and derive the leading quadratic term. (c) Compute the quadratic approximations for \\(q=0.2\\) and \\(q=0.5\\) with \\(h=0.02\\). Which state produces greater information mismatch for the same probability change? (d) For a three-outcome reference \\(\\mathbf q\\), explain why a tangent displacement must satisfy \\(h_A+h_B+h_C=0\\)."],"quiz":[["KL divergence \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is the difference between",["cross-entropy CE(Q,P) and Shannon entropy H(Q)","the two entropy values H(Q) and H(P)","two Euclidean lengths","probability and variance"],0,"D(Q||P)=CE(Q,P)−H(Q), the excess expected log loss using P when Q generates observations."],["Which statement about KL divergence is correct?",["It is symmetric for all distributions.","It is never negative but is generally asymmetric.","It always satisfies the triangle inequality.","It equals Shannon entropy."],1,"Gibbs' inequality gives nonnegativity, but reversing its arguments generally changes the value."],["If \\(q_i>0\\) but \\(p_i=0\\) for some outcome, then \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is",["zero","always less than one","infinite","negative"],2,"The proposed model assigns zero probability to an outcome that can occur under Q."],["For \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)\\) with interior q, which derivative is correct?",["\\(\\frac{x-q}{x(1-x)}\\)","\\(\\frac{q-x}{x(1-x)}\\)","\\(q\\ln x\\)","\\(x(1-x)\\)"],0,"Differentiate the two logarithmic terms and combine them over x(1−x)."],["For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) and \\(q=3/4\\), the KL Lyapunov derivative is",["\\(4(x-q)^2\\)","\\(-4(x-q)^2\\)","\\(\\ln(x/q)\\)","\\(0\\) for every x"],1,"Use the chain rule: V′x′=(x−q)(3−4x)=−4(x−q)²."],["Along this stable coexistence solution, Shannon entropy",["must increase at all times","equals KL divergence","can increase then decrease even while KL decreases","is identically zero"],2,"Starting below x=1/2, the entropy rises to ln2 then falls as x approaches q=3/4."],["The leading term of \\(D(q\\|q+h)\\) for small h is",["\\(\\frac{h}{q(1-q)}\\)","\\(\\frac{h^2}{2q(1-q)}\\)","\\(h^2q(1-q)\\)","\\(q+h\\)"],1,"At equality, KL and its first derivative vanish; the second derivative is 1/[q(1−q)]."],["Which statement best describes the connection to Week 7?",["KL itself is a Euclidean distance.","The KL quadratic term defines a tangent-space metric on positive distributions.","All points in the simplex have the same quadratic coefficient.","The metric is unrelated to probabilities."],1,"The local quadratic term is positive definite on tangent vectors and produces Fisher information geometry."]],"sections":[["From entropy to relative entropy","If the actual distribution is \\(Q=(q_i)\\) and the predictive distribution is \\(P=(p_i)\\), the expected logarithmic loss is \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)=-\\sum_iq_i\\ln p_i\\). Since \\(H(Q)=-\\sum_iq_i\\ln q_i\\), their difference is \\(D(Q\\|P)=\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)-H(Q)=\\sum_iq_i\\ln(q_i/p_i)\\). The order matters: the generating reference distribution is first. Divergence is nonnegative, but generally asymmetric. If some \\(q_i>0\\) and \\(p_i=0\\), it is infinite."],["Nonnegativity and the simplex","The inequality \\(\\ln u\\leq u-1\\) implies \\(D(Q\\|P)\\geq\\sum_i(q_i-p_i)=0\\) for positive distributions. Equality occurs precisely when the distributions agree. For fixed positive Q, \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is a scalar landscape over the simplex with its minimum at P=Q."],["A Lyapunov function for a replicator equation","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(a-bx)\\), take \\(b>0\\) and \\(q=a/b\\in(0,1)\\). Let \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)=q\\ln(q/x)+(1-q)\\ln((1-q)/(1-x))\\). Differentiating gives \\(V'(x)=(x-q)/[x(1-x)]\\) and hence \\(\\dot V=(x-q)(a-bx)=-b(x-q)^2\\leq0\\). This proves the strict decay of KL mismatch away from the attracting equilibrium; it need not coincide with increasing Shannon entropy."],["The local quadratic form","For a small change \\(h\\) around interior Bernoulli state q, Taylor expansion gives \\(D(q\\|q+h)=h^2/[2q(1-q)]+O(h^3)\\). More generally, a small tangent displacement \\(\\mathbf h\\) with \\(\\sum_i h_i=0\\) satisfies \\(D(\\mathbf q\\|\\mathbf q+\\mathbf h)=\\frac12\\sum_i h_i^2/q_i+O(\\|\\mathbf h\\|^3)\\). The quadratic term is the geometric object that becomes the Fisher information metric in Week 7."]],"key":"\\frac{d}{dt}D(q\\|x(t))=-b(x-q)^2,\\qquad D(q\\|q+h)=\\frac{h^2}{2q(1-q)}+O(h^3)","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), the equilibrium is \\(q=3/4\\). The function \\(V(x)=\\frac34\\ln[(3/4)/x]+\\frac14\\ln[(1/4)/(1-x)]\\) decreases along every nonstationary interior trajectory, since \\(\\dot V=-4(x-3/4)^2\\). Unlike Shannon entropy, this KL divergence is guaranteed to decay in this model.","readingPath":"readings/week06.html","demoPath":"demos/week06.html","labPath":"labs/week06.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week06.html"},{"week":7,"title":"Fisher Information and Local Geometry","subtitle":"From local KL divergence to lengths, angles, and speeds","summary":"Construct the Fisher metric from KL curvature and score functions, measure Bernoulli lengths and categorical angles, and interpret replicator speed as fitness variance.","reading":"readings/week07.html","demo":"demos/week07.html","lab":["Return to KL divergence. For interior Bernoulli probability p and small displacement h, write \\(D(p\\|p+h)\\). (a) Differentiate once and twice with respect to h, evaluating both derivatives at \\(h=0\\). (b) Explain why the first-order term vanishes and derive \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\). (c) Independently derive the same Fisher coefficient as the expected squared Bernoulli score. Give a sentence explaining why the two derivations agree conceptually.","Use the Bernoulli Fisher ruler in the demo. (a) Calculate \\(g(p)\\) at \\(p=0.1,0.25,0.5,0.9\\). (b) Evaluate the exact Fisher distances for the two moves \\(0.10\\to0.15\\) and \\(0.50\\to0.55\\). Compare them with the first-order estimates \\(|dp|/\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\). (c) Starting with \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\), substitute \\(p=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\) to prove \\(ds^2=d\\theta^2\\). Why is the total Fisher length of the open Bernoulli interval \\(\\pi\\)?","At \\(q=(0.5,0.3,0.2)\\), consider the tangent vectors \\(u=(0.1,-0.05,-0.05)\\) and \\(v=(0,0.1,-0.1)\\). (a) Verify that both are tangent to the simplex. (b) Compute their Euclidean dot product, the Fisher dot product, their Fisher squared norms, and the Fisher angle. (c) Use the categorical demo to compare these two notions of angle. (d) In independent coordinates \\(x=p_A,y=p_B\\), derive the \\(2\\times2\\) Fisher metric matrix.","Take two strategies with constant fitnesses \\(f_A=2,\\ f_B=0\\) and initial distribution \\(p=(1/4,3/4)\\). (a) Compute mean fitness, fitness variance, and the replicator velocity. (b) Compute \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2\\) directly from the metric and compare it with the variance. (c) Derive the general identity \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\) for three or more strategies. (d) Use the demo to see how speed changes as the trajectory approaches fixation.","Suppose a Bernoulli fitness advantage depends on p as \\(\\Delta(p)=3-4p\\). Define the potential \\(\\Phi(p)=\\int_0^p(3-4s)\\,ds\\). (a) Compute \\(\\Phi(p)\\) and its ordinary derivative. (b) Multiply by the inverse Fisher metric to obtain the natural gradient. (c) Show that its flow reproduces the Week 1 replicator equation. (d) Explain the difference between a critical point of the potential, a Fisher-gradient equilibrium, and a maximum of Shannon entropy."],"quiz":[["For a positive categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p\\), the Fisher squared norm of a tangent vector u is",["\\(\\sum_i u_i^2/p_i\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_i u_i^2\\)","\\((\\sum_i u_i)^2\\)","\\(\\sum_i u_i\\)"],0,"The local quadratic KL term gives the Fisher inner product weighted by 1/p_i."],["For Bernoulli probability p, the Fisher information coefficient is",["\\(p(1-p)\\)","\\(1/[p(1-p)]\\)","\\(p^2\\)","\\(1\\)"],1,"Evaluate the expected squared Bernoulli score, or the second derivative of KL."],["At \\(p=1/2\\), the Bernoulli Fisher coefficient is",["1","2","4","8"],2,"1/[0.5(1−0.5)]=4."],["Which coordinate makes the Bernoulli Fisher metric equal to \\(d\\theta^2\\)?",["\\(\\theta=p^2\\)","\\(\\theta=\\ln p\\)","\\(\\theta=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\)","\\(\\theta=p(1-p)\\)"],2,"The substitution p=sin²(θ/2) cancels the metric coefficient."],["For two monotone Bernoulli states a and b, intrinsic Fisher distance is",["the KL divergence D(a||b)","\\(2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\)","\\(|a-b|\\)","\\(1/[a(1-a)]\\)"],1,"Integrate the Fisher line element dp/√[p(1−p)]."],["For a three-category tangent vector u, which constraint holds?",["\\(\\sum_i u_i=0\\)","\\(\\sum_i u_i=1\\)","\\(u_i\\geq0\\) for all i","\\(\\sum_i u_i^2=1\\)"],0,"Differentiate the normalization condition ∑p_i=1."],["Along replicator dynamics, \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2\\) equals",["mean fitness","Shannon entropy","fitness variance","the entropy production rate"],2,"Substituting p_i'=p_i(f_i−mean f) produces the weighted variance of fitness."],["For Bernoulli potential \\(\\Phi(p)\\), its Fisher natural gradient has scalar component",["\\(\\Phi'(p)\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\Phi'(p)\\)","\\(\\Phi'(p)/[p(1-p)]\\)","\\(p+\\Phi'(p)\\)"],1,"The Fisher gradient multiplies the ordinary derivative by the inverse metric g⁻¹=p(1−p)."]],"sections":[["The KL quadratic term defines length","For a positive reference distribution \\(\\mathbf p\\), a small tangent displacement \\(\\mathbf h\\) with \\(\\sum_i h_i=0\\) obeys \\(D(\\mathbf p\\|\\mathbf p+\\mathbf h)=\\frac12\\sum_i h_i^2/p_i+O(\\|\\mathbf h\\|^3)\\). Thus the Fisher inner product on tangent vectors is \\(g_{\\mathbf p}(\\mathbf u,\\mathbf v)=\\sum_i u_iv_i/p_i\\). It measures local distinguishability, not a global KL distance."],["Bernoulli information and intrinsic distance","For \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), Fisher information equals \\(I(p)=E_p[(\\partial_p\\ln P_p(X))^2]=1/[p(1-p)]\\). Hence \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\), and the arc coordinate \\(\\theta=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\) satisfies \\(ds^2=d\\theta^2\\). Consequently \\(d_F(a,b)=2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\). The metric blows up at the coordinate boundaries, but the distance to a boundary is finite."],["Lengths and angles in the categorical simplex","For \\(\\mathbf p=(x,y,1-x-y)\\), a tangent vector obeys \\(h_1+h_2+h_3=0\\), so the metric matrix in x,y coordinates is \\(G(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}1/x+1/z&1/z\\\\1/z&1/y+1/z\\end{pmatrix}\\) with \\(z=1-x-y\\). Two tangent vectors need not have the same Fisher angle as their Euclidean angle; use \\(\\cos\\alpha_F=g_p(u,v)/[\\|u\\|_F\\|v\\|_F]\\)."],["Replicator speed and the natural gradient","The squared Fisher speed of an interior probability curve is \\(\\sum_i(p_i')^2/p_i\\). Along replicator dynamics \\(p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\bar f)\\), this becomes \\(\\sum_i p_i(f_i-\\bar f)^2=\\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(f)\\). For Bernoulli, the Fisher gradient of a potential \\(\\Phi\\) is \\(p(1-p)\\Phi'(p)\\), so a constant fitness advantage gives the logistic equation as a Fisher gradient flow."]],"key":"g_{\\mathbf p}(u,v)=\\sum_i\\frac{u_iv_i}{p_i},\\qquad ds^2=\\frac{dp^2}{p(1-p)},\\qquad \\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(f)","example":"At Bernoulli probability \\(p=1/2\\), the Fisher coefficient is 4; at \\(p=1/10\\), it is \\(100/9\\approx11.11\\). The Fisher distance from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) is \\(2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\). For replicator fitnesses \\(f=(2,0)\\) and \\(p=(1/4,3/4)\\), both fitness variance and squared Fisher speed are \\(3/4\\).","readingPath":"readings/week07.html","demoPath":"demos/week07.html","labPath":"labs/week07.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week07.html"},{"week":8,"title":"Square-Root Coordinates and Spherical Geometry","subtitle":"The Fisher simplex becomes a round sphere","summary":"Derive the square-root spherical isometry, calculate geodesic distance, and compare great-circle paths with ordinary probability mixtures.","reading":"readings/week08.html","demo":"demos/week08.html","lab":["Let \\(\\mathbf p=(p_A,p_B,p_C)\\) be an interior categorical state. (a) Show directly that \\(Q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i}\\) satisfies \\(\\|Q\\|^2=4\\). (b) Calculate \\(dQ_i\\) and prove \\(\\sum_i(dQ_i)^2=\\sum_i(dp_i)^2/p_i\\). (c) Explain why the condition \\(dp_A+dp_B+dp_C=0\\) means the image velocity is tangent to the sphere. (d) In the demo, move an endpoint around the simplex and compare the positive spherical coordinates to its probabilities.","Take interior states \\(P=(0.5,0.3,0.2)\\) and \\(R=(0.2,0.5,0.3)\\). (a) Calculate \\(B(P,R)=\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\). (b) Calculate the central angle \\(\\alpha=\\arccos B\\) and the Fisher distance \\(d_F=2\\alpha\\). (c) Calculate the chord length \\(\\|2\\sqrt P-2\\sqrt R\\|\\). (d) Compare all three quantities in the demo. Which of them measures the shortest route *on* the statistical manifold?","Use the same endpoints P and R. (a) Derive the great-circle interpolation \\(Q(t)=[\\sin((1-t)\\alpha)Q_0+\\sin(t\\alpha)Q_1]/\\sin\\alpha\\), with the appropriate sine arguments. (b) Convert its midpoint back to probabilities and verify the components sum to 1. (c) Calculate the midpoint of the straight mixture \\(M(t)=(1-t)P+tR\\). (d) In the demo, compare the two paths; use the numerical Fisher-length readout to decide which is shorter, and explain why.","For Bernoulli \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), show that the square-root vectors lie on a quarter-circle of radius 2. (a) Derive \\(\\theta(p)=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\). (b) Compute the Fisher distance between \\(p=1/4\\) and \\(p=3/4\\). (c) Take \\(p\\to0\\) and \\(r\\to1\\), and explain why the limiting distance is \\(\\pi\\) even though \\(1/[p(1-p)]\\) diverges near the endpoints. (d) Use the Bernoulli mode to compare a constant-speed Fisher geodesic with linear interpolation of the probabilities.","In the categorical demo, set the endpoints near two different vertices, then move them toward the pure states \\((1,0,0)\\) and \\((0,1,0)\\). (a) Estimate how the Fisher geodesic distance approaches \\(\\pi\\). (b) Show that the corresponding Euclidean distance in ordinary probability coordinates approaches \\(\\sqrt2\\). (c) Compute the Bhattacharyya coefficient and the limiting chord length in square-root coordinates. (d) Why does KL divergence between the two limiting pure states become infinite even though Fisher distance remains finite?"],"quiz":[["For \\(q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i}\\), the constraint \\(\\sum_i p_i=1\\) becomes",["\\(\\sum_iq_i=2\\)","\\(\\sum_iq_i^2=4\\)","\\(\\sum_iq_i^2=1\\)","\\(\\sum_iq_i=1\\)"],1,"Squaring gives q_i²=4p_i, so their sum is 4."],["Which metric is induced by the square-root coordinates on the probability simplex?",["A Lorentzian metric","The Euclidean metric restricted to the radius-2 sphere","A taxicab metric","An identically zero metric"],1,"Differentiation gives dq_i²=dp_i²/p_i."],["The Bhattacharyya coefficient \\(B(P,R)\\) is",["\\(\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_ir_i^2\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_i\\ln(p_i/r_i)\\)","\\(\\sum_i(p_i-r_i)^2\\)"],0,"It is the dot product of the two unit square-root probability vectors."],["The intrinsic Fisher distance between two positive categorical distributions is",["\\(\\|P-R\\|\\)","\\(D(P\\|R)\\)","\\(2\\arccos\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\)","\\(1-\\sum_i p_ir_i\\)"],2,"It is the radius 2 times the central angle between square-root vectors."],["On a round sphere, shortest intrinsic paths between non-antipodal endpoints follow",["straight chords through the ball","great-circle arcs","parallels of latitude only","arbitrary straight probability mixtures"],1,"Geodesics on the round sphere are arcs of great circles."],["A straight interpolation of probabilities between two interior states",["is always a Fisher geodesic at constant speed","stays in the simplex but is generally not a Fisher geodesic","leaves the simplex immediately","has zero Fisher length"],1,"Linear mixtures follow chords in the flat simplex picture, but the Fisher metric is spherical."],["The limiting Fisher distance between two different pure categorical states is",["zero","infinite","\\(\\sqrt2\\)","\\(\\pi\\)"],3,"Their square-root vectors are perpendicular radius-2 vectors."],["For a three-outcome categorical Fisher simplex, its Gaussian curvature is",["zero","\\(\\frac14\\)","\\(1\\)","\\(4\\)"],1,"The space is isometric to a patch of a round sphere of radius 2, with curvature 1/R²."]],"sections":[["From the Fisher quadratic form to a sphere","For positive probabilities \\(\\mathbf p\\) with \\(\\sum_i p_i=1\\), the Fisher metric is \\(ds_F^2=\\sum_i dp_i^2/p_i\\) on tangent vectors. Set \\(q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i}\\). Then \\(dq_i=dp_i/\\sqrt{p_i}\\) and hence \\(\\sum_i dq_i^2=ds_F^2\\). Normalization becomes \\(\\sum_iq_i^2=4\\), so the categorical simplex interior is isometric to the positive orthant of a radius-2 sphere."],["Spherical distance and the Bhattacharyya coefficient","For distributions P and R, define the Bhattacharyya coefficient \\(B(P,R)=\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\). It lies in \\([0,1]\\). The square-root vectors make central angle \\(\\alpha=\\arccos B(P,R)\\), so the intrinsic Fisher distance is \\(d_F(P,R)=2\\alpha=2\\arccos(\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i})\\). This is the length of a great-circle arc, not the ambient chord length and not KL divergence."],["Great-circle interpolation versus mixture","For distinct interior endpoints \\(Q=2\\sqrt P\\) and \\(S=2\\sqrt R\\), the constant-speed spherical geodesic is \\(Q(t)=[\\sin((1-t)\\alpha)/\\sin\\alpha]Q+[\\sin(t\\alpha)/\\sin\\alpha]S\\). Its probabilities are \\(p_i(t)=(Q_i(t)/2)^2\\). Contrast it with the mixture \\(M(t)=(1-t)P+tR\\), whose Fisher length is \\(\\int_0^1\\sqrt{\\sum_i(r_i-p_i)^2/M_i(t)}\\,dt\\). The mixture is generally longer."],["The finite boundary and the geometric conclusion","For Bernoulli \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), the spherical arc coordinate is \\(\\theta(p)=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\), reproducing \\(d_F(p,r)=|\\theta(p)-\\theta(r)|\\). A coordinate coefficient may blow up near the boundary even though the boundary lies at finite Fisher distance. The metric completion is the closed positive spherical orthant; distinct pure categorical distributions have Fisher separation \\(\\pi\\). For three outcomes the Fisher simplex has constant positive Gaussian curvature \\(1/4\\)."]],"key":"q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i},\\quad \\sum_iq_i^2=4,\\quad d_F(P,R)=2\\arccos\\left(\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\right)","example":"For \\(P=(1,0,0)\\) and \\(R=(0,1,0)\\) in the metric completion, \\(B(P,R)=0\\) and \\(d_F(P,R)=2\\arccos0=\\pi\\). For positive interior states, the geodesic is obtained by spherical linear interpolation of their square-root vectors and squaring the coordinates to return to the probability simplex.","readingPath":"readings/week08.html","demoPath":"demos/week08.html","labPath":"labs/week08.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week08.html"},{"week":9,"title":"Natural Gradients and Potential-Driven Evolution","subtitle":"When replicator dynamics climb a Fisher potential","summary":"Derive the natural gradient, link potential ascent with fitness variance, and compare softmax, KL, entropy, and cyclic replicator systems.","reading":"readings/week09.html","demo":"demos/week09.html","lab":["Let \\(ds^2=g(x)dx^2\\). (a) Derive \\(\\operatorname{grad}_g\\Phi=\\Phi'/g\\) from the defining inner-product identity. (b) For Bernoulli \\(g=1/[x(1-x)]\\) and \\(\\Phi=3x-2x^2\\), write the natural-gradient ODE and identify all equilibria. (c) Derive \\(\\dot\\Phi=x(1-x)(3-4x)^2\\). (d) Use the binary demo to compare the potential curve and time evolution.","At \\(p=(1/3,1/3,1/3)\\), let \\(\\Phi(p)=2p_A+p_B\\). (a) Find the partial derivatives and their probability-weighted mean. (b) Compute the Fisher gradient and verify that its components sum to zero. (c) Compute potential ascent rate and fitness variance, showing they agree. (d) Examine the linear-potential demo and relate its trajectory to Week 5's exponential normalization.","Fix target \\(q=(0.3,0.4,0.3)\\) and start at \\(p(0)=(0.65,0.2,0.15)\\). (a) Compute the derivatives of \\(\\Phi=-D(q\\|p)\\). (b) Show the natural-gradient flow satisfies \\(p'=q-p\\). (c) Solve explicitly and calculate \\(p(1)\\). (d) Derive \\(-\\dot D(q\\|p)=\\sum_i(q_i-p_i)^2/p_i\\). (e) Compare this mixture path to the Fisher geodesic toward q using the demo; why are they different?","Let the potential be Shannon entropy \\(H(p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i\\). (a) Compute its partial derivatives, p-weighted mean and Fisher gradient. (b) Show \\((\\ln(p_i/p_j))'=-\\ln(p_i/p_j)\\) and derive \\(p_i(t)\\propto p_i(0)^{e^{-t}}\\). (c) Find the limiting distribution for strictly positive initial probabilities. (d) Explore the entropy preset and contrast the monotone entropy graph with the Week 1 coexistence example.","For balanced cyclic fitnesses \\(f=(p_B-p_C,p_C-p_A,p_A-p_B)\\), (a) verify mean fitness is zero. (b) Show \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\ln(p_Ap_Bp_C)=0\\) along interior trajectories. (c) Explore a cyclic orbit in the demo and explain why it cannot strictly increase a globally single-valued potential. (d) Show why \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\) remains valid without a potential."],"quiz":[["For \\(ds^2=g(x)dx^2\\), the metric gradient of \\(\\Phi\\) has scalar component",["\\(\\Phi'(x)/g(x)\\)","\\(g(x)\\Phi'(x)\\)","\\(\\Phi''(x)\\)","\\(g(x)+\\Phi(x)\\)"],0,"By definition the metric converts the differential covector into the gradient vector using its inverse."],["The Bernoulli Fisher natural-gradient multiplier is",["\\(1/[x(1-x)]\\)","\\(x(1-x)\\)","\\(1\\)","\\(x+1\\)"],1,"The Bernoulli Fisher metric coefficient is 1/[x(1−x)], whose inverse is x(1−x)."],["The Fisher gradient of a simplex potential has i-th component",["\\(p_i(\\partial_i\\Phi-\\sum_jp_j\\partial_j\\Phi)\\)","\\(\\partial_i\\Phi\\)","\\(p_i+\\partial_i\\Phi\\)","\\(\\sum_jp_j\\partial_j\\Phi\\)"],0,"The p-weighted mean subtraction keeps the gradient vector tangent to the normalized simplex."],["Along an upward Fisher-gradient replicator flow, \\(\\dot\\Phi\\) equals",["\\(-\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\)","\\(\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\geq0\\)","\\(H(p)\\) for every potential","\\(0\\) everywhere"],1,"Potential growth is squared Fisher speed, equal to fitness variance."],["If \\(\\Phi(p)=-D(q\\|p)\\), the Fisher gradient flow is",["\\(p'=q-p\\)","\\(p'=p-q\\)","\\(p'=0\\) everywhere","\\(p_i'=q_ip_i\\)"],0,"The partials are q_i/p_i, and their p-weighted mean is one."],["The Fisher-gradient flow of Shannon entropy from a positive interior distribution approaches",["a vertex","the uniform distribution","a nonstationary closed orbit","a negative probability state"],1,"All log probability ratios decay exponentially toward zero."],["Why is a nonstationary cyclic periodic orbit incompatible with strict gradient ascent?",["The Fisher metric is undefined throughout the interior","A strictly increasing single-valued potential cannot return to its initial value","Every cyclic orbit has zero fitness variance","Normalization fails"],1,"Returning to the same state requires the same potential value, contradicting strict monotonic increase."],["Which statement correctly distinguishes Fisher geodesics and natural-gradient paths?",["Every natural-gradient trajectory minimizes Fisher length","Geodesics minimize length; natural-gradient paths follow scalar objectives","Geodesics exist only for Euclidean metrics","Natural gradients must produce closed orbits"],1,"A shortest path and a potential-driven flow solve different geometric problems."]],"sections":[["A gradient depends on the metric","With line element \\(ds^2=g(x)dx^2\\), the metric gradient has component \\(\\Phi'(x)/g(x)\\). For Bernoulli Fisher geometry this becomes \\(x(1-x)\\Phi'(x)\\). Taking \\(\\Phi(x)=3x-2x^2\\) recovers the Week 1 equation \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\)."],["Natural gradients on the simplex","For a positive probability vector, \\(g_p(u,v)=\\sum_i u_iv_i/p_i\\) and \\(\\sum_i v_i=0\\). Hence \\((\\operatorname{grad}_F\\Phi)_i=p_i(\\partial_i\\Phi-\\sum_jp_j\\partial_j\\Phi)\\). The mean subtraction makes the gradient tangent and shows that adding a constant to every fitness does not change the motion."],["Gradient ascent and fitness variance","For potential fitness \\(f_i=\\partial_i\\Phi\\), the replicator system is the Fisher gradient flow. Along it, \\(\\dot\\Phi=\\|\\operatorname{grad}_F\\Phi\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\geq0\\). A linear potential \\(\\Phi=\\sum_i c_ip_i\\) yields the exact exponential-normalization solution \\(p_i(t)=p_i(0)e^{c_it}/\\sum_jp_j(0)e^{c_jt}\\)."],["KL, entropy and cyclic dynamics","The potential \\(-D(q\\|p)\\) gives \\(p_i'=q_i-p_i\\), so \\(p(t)=q+e^{-t}(p_0-q)\\), an ordinary mixture segment, generally not a Fisher geodesic. An entropy potential \\(H(p)\\) yields \\(p_i(t)\\propto p_i(0)^{e^{-t}}\\) and converges to uniform. Balanced cyclic competition conserves \\(p_Ap_Bp_C\\) and has closed nonstationary orbits, impossible for strict potential ascent."]],"key":"(\\operatorname{grad}_F\\Phi)_i=p_i\\left(\\partial_i\\Phi-\\sum_jp_j\\partial_j\\Phi\\right),\\qquad \\dot\\Phi=\\operatorname{Var}_{p}(f)","example":"For \\(\\Phi(x)=3x-2x^2\\), the Fisher gradient gives \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) and \\(\\dot\\Phi=x(1-x)(3-4x)^2\\geq0\\). For \\(\\Phi(p)=-D(q\\|p)\\), the Fisher gradient is \\(p'=q-p\\): a straight mixture trajectory that generally is not a Fisher geodesic.","readingPath":"readings/week09.html","demoPath":"demos/week09.html","labPath":"labs/week09.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week09.html"},{"week":10,"title":"Gaussian Families and Hyperbolic Geometry","subtitle":"A negatively curved Fisher manifold","summary":"Derive the Fisher metric of normal distributions, explore hyperbolic geodesics in the mean–standard-deviation half-plane, and compare Fisher distance with KL.","reading":"readings/week10.html","demo":"demos/week10.html","lab":["Start from the normal density \\(p(x;\\mu,\\sigma)\\), with \\(\\sigma>0\\). (a) Find the log density and compute the score derivatives with respect to \\(\\mu\\) and \\(\\sigma\\). (b) Use the Gaussian central moments \\(E[(X-\\mu)^2]=\\sigma^2\\), \\(E[(X-\\mu)^3]=0\\), and \\(E[(X-\\mu)^4]=3\\sigma^4\\) to calculate the three entries of the Fisher information matrix. (c) Explain why the off-diagonal entry vanishes. (d) Write the resulting line element.","For the Gaussian Fisher metric \\(ds_F^2=(d\\mu^2+2\\,d\\sigma^2)/\\sigma^2\\): (a) Substitute \\(u=\\mu/\\sqrt2,\\ v=\\sigma\\) and obtain the scaled hyperbolic upper-half-plane metric. (b) Explain why the restriction \\(v>0\\) matters. (c) State the Gaussian Fisher curvature and contrast its sign with the three-category Fisher sphere from Week 8. (d) In the explorer, move vertically and horizontally from the same Gaussian state; compare the predicted local costs of the two motions.","Choose \\(P=N(0,1^2)\\) and \\(R=N(0,2^2)\\). (a) Compute the Fisher distance by integrating along the vertical geodesic. (b) Repeat for \\(N(0,2^2)\\) and \\(N(0,4^2)\\). (c) Explain the role of logarithmic ratios. (d) Show why \\(N(0,\\sigma^2)\\) moves infinitely far from \\(N(0,1^2)\\) as \\(\\sigma\\to0^+\\), and contrast this with the Bernoulli boundary in Week 8.","Let \\(P=N(-2,1^2)\\) and \\(R=N(2,1^2)\\). (a) Convert their parameters into upper-half-plane coordinates. (b) Calculate their exact Fisher–Rao distance using the arcosh formula. (c) Compute the Fisher length of the straight parameter path with \\(\\sigma=1\\). (d) Use the demo to inspect the curved geodesic and its higher standard deviation between the two means. (e) Explain why moving through a temporarily broader Gaussian distribution can reduce total Fisher length.","Take \\(P=N(0,1^2)\\) and \\(R=N(1,2^2)\\). (a) Calculate \\(D(P\\|R)\\) and \\(D(R\\|P)\\). (b) Calculate their Fisher distance and verify its symmetry. (c) Use the explorer to compare all three values, distinguishing cross-entropy loss from intrinsic length. (d) Summarize, in a short paragraph, how Weeks 1–10 connect replicator dynamics, probability models, entropy and KL, Fisher geometry, natural gradients, the categorical sphere, and the Gaussian hyperbolic plane."],"quiz":[["For \\(N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\) parameterized by \\((\\mu,\\sigma)\\), the Fisher information matrix is",["\\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/\\sigma^2,2/\\sigma^2)\\)","\\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma^2,\\sigma^2)\\)","\\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,1)\\)","\\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/\\sigma,1/\\sigma)\\)"],0,"Gaussian score products give information 1/σ² for mean and 2/σ² for standard deviation."],["The score derivative \\(\\partial_\\mu\\ln p(X;\\mu,\\sigma)\\) is",["\\(-1/\\sigma\\)","\\((X-\\mu)/\\sigma^2\\)","\\((X-\\mu)^2/\\sigma^3\\)","\\(1/\\mu\\)"],1,"Differentiate the quadratic term in the Gaussian log density with respect to the mean."],["With \\(u=\\mu/\\sqrt2\\), \\(v=\\sigma\\), the Fisher metric becomes",["\\(du^2+dv^2\\)","\\((du^2+dv^2)/v\\)","\\(2(du^2+dv^2)/v^2\\)","\\(2(du^2-dv^2)/v^2\\)"],2,"The transformed line element is a constant multiple of the Poincaré upper-half-plane metric."],["The curvature of the two-parameter Gaussian Fisher manifold is",["\\(+1/4\\)","\\(-1/2\\)","\\(0\\)","\\(-2\\)"],1,"Scaling the curvature −1 of the usual hyperbolic metric by the factor 2 gives −1/2."],["In the upper-half-plane model, nonvertical geodesics are",["Euclidean straight lines of any slope","circles tangent to the top boundary","semicircles orthogonal to the boundary v=0","hyperbolas centered on the origin"],2,"Hyperbolic geodesics are vertical lines or semicircles meeting v=0 at right angles."],["At fixed mean, the Fisher distance between standard deviations \\(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2\\) is",["\\(|\\sigma_2-\\sigma_1|\\)","\\(\\sqrt2|\\ln(\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1)|\\)","\\(D(P\\|R)\\)","\\((\\sigma_2-\\sigma_1)^2\\)"],1,"Integrate √2 dσ/σ along the vertical geodesic."],["As Gaussian standard deviation σ approaches zero, the Fisher distance from a fixed positive σ becomes",["zero","a finite constant","infinite","identically π"],2,"Vertical Fisher length grows like the absolute logarithm of σ."],["Which statement distinguishes Gaussian KL divergence and Gaussian Fisher distance?",["Both are necessarily asymmetric","KL is a directed divergence; Fisher distance is symmetric and measures geodesic length","KL is always a spherical arc length","Fisher distance equals a Euclidean parameter difference"],1,"KL gives excess expected log loss in a chosen direction, whereas Fisher distance is a symmetric intrinsic metric."]],"sections":[["Gaussian scores and Fisher information","The density of \\(N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\) is \\(p(x;\\mu,\\sigma)=e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/(2\\sigma^2)}/(\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi})\\). Its log-likelihood scores are \\(\\partial_\\mu\\ell=(x-\\mu)/\\sigma^2\\) and \\(\\partial_\\sigma\\ell=-1/\\sigma+(x-\\mu)^2/\\sigma^3\\). Their expected outer products give \\(G(\\mu,\\sigma)=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma^{-2},2\\sigma^{-2})\\)."],["The upper-half-plane Fisher metric","The Gaussian Fisher line element is \\(ds_F^2=(d\\mu^2+2d\\sigma^2)/\\sigma^2\\). Set \\(u=\\mu/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(v=\\sigma\\), obtaining \\(ds_F^2=2(du^2+dv^2)/v^2\\). This is twice the standard Poincaré upper-half-plane metric and has constant curvature \\(-1/2\\), contrasting with the positive curvature \\(1/4\\) of the three-outcome categorical Fisher sphere."],["Hyperbolic geodesics, distances and paths","Geodesics are vertical lines or semicircles orthogonal to the boundary \\(v=0\\). For any two Gaussians the Fisher distance is \\(d_F=\\sqrt2\\,\\operatorname{arcosh}\\left(1+\\frac{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2+2(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)^2}{4\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}\\right)\\). With the same mean, this reduces to \\(\\sqrt2|\\ln(\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1)|\\). A straight interpolation of mean and standard deviation generally has greater Fisher length."],["KL, boundary, and the final geometric comparison","For two Gaussians P and R, \\(D(P\\|R)=\\ln(\\sigma_R/\\sigma_P)+[\\sigma_P^2+(\\mu_P-\\mu_R)^2]/(2\\sigma_R^2)-1/2\\). This divergence is directed, whereas Fisher distance is symmetric. The Gaussian boundary \\(\\sigma=0\\) is infinitely far away in Fisher geometry, unlike the finite-distance boundary of a categorical simplex. The statistical family determines which classical geometry arises."]],"key":"G(\\mu,\\sigma)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sigma^{-2}&0\\\\0&2\\sigma^{-2}\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad ds_F^2=2\\frac{du^2+dv^2}{v^2},\\qquad K=-\\frac12","example":"The Gaussian distributions \\(N(0,1^2)\\) and \\(N(0,2^2)\\) lie on a vertical hyperbolic geodesic. Their Fisher distance is \\(\\sqrt2\\ln2\\approx0.9803\\). The same distance separates \\(N(0,2^2)\\) and \\(N(0,4^2)\\): Fisher length depends on the ratio of standard deviations along a fixed-mean path.","readingPath":"readings/week10.html","demoPath":"demos/week10.html","labPath":"labs/week10.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week10.html"}];
